        You will be given the root cause of a bug, the commit message and the corresponding patch.
        You need to extract all code statements that lead to the bug from the patch, based on the root cause and the commit message.

        Root cause:
        The bug was caused by the incorrect handling of spurious interrupts in the `mpc8xx_get_irq()` function, where it returned a value from `irq_linear_revmap(...)` instead of returning 0 when no valid interrupt was detected. This led to confusion in the interrupt handling logic, as IRQ 0 is a valid hardware interrupt, and the function should indicate the absence of an interrupt by returning 0 directly.

        Commit message:
        powerpc/8xx: fix mpc8xx_get_irq() return on no irq

IRQ 0 is a valid HW interrupt. So get_irq() shall return 0 when
there is no irq, instead of returning irq_linear_revmap(... ,0)

Fixes: f2a0bd3753dad ("[POWERPC] 8xx: powerpc port of core CPM PIC")
Signed-off-by: Christophe Leroy <christophe.leroy@c-s.fr>
Signed-off-by: Michael Ellerman <mpe@ellerman.id.au>  

        Patch:

 unsigned int mpc8xx_get_irq(void)
 {
 	int irq;

 	/* For MPC8xx, read the SIVEC register and shift the bits down
 	 * to get the irq number.
 	 */
 	irq = in_be32(&siu_reg->sc_sivec) >> 26;

 	if (irq == PIC_VEC_SPURRIOUS)
-		irq = 0;
+		return 0;

         return irq_linear_revmap(mpc8xx_pic_host, irq);

 }



        Provide a response strictly in the following format:
        Code statements:
        <text1>
        <text2>
        ...
        <textN>
